# Python Project Bootstrap

Set up new Python projects or migrate existing ones to a modern 2026+ tooling stack.

## The Stack

| Layer | Tool | Replaces |
|---|---|---|
| Package manager | **uv** | pip, virtualenv, pip-tools, tox |
| Build backend | **hatchling** | setuptools, setup.cfg, setup.py |
| Formatter | **ruff format** | black, isort |
| Linter | **ruff check** | flake8, bandit, isort, pyupgrade, dozens of plugins |
| Type checker | **pyright** (strict) | mypy |
| Dead code | **vulture** | manual review |
| Task runner | **just** | tox, Makefile |
| WSGI server | **granian** | uWSGI, gunicorn |
| Tests | **pytest** + xdist + testmon + timeout | unittest, nose |
| Dev deps | **`[dependency-groups]`** (PEP 735) | `[project.optional-dependencies]` |
| Issue tracking | **dcat** (dogcat) | GitHub Issues, Jira, plain TODO lists |

**Do NOT use:** black, isort, mypy, bandit, tox, setuptools, `pip install`, `npm`, `[project.optional-dependencies]` for dev deps.

- **Python version support:** templates target the single pinned version (`requires-python` in pyproject.toml). If the project is a library that must support multiple versions, test them via a CI matrix — don't loosen the pin.
- **CLI entry points:** for command-line tools, expose commands via `[project.scripts]` (see core-templates.md).
- **No pre-commit:** pre-commit is intentionally not used — the justfile recipes and CI cover formatting/linting. Do not re-add it on re-bootstrap.

